Make the gossip peer-connect task abortable, so session close can finish.

THE SYMPTOM. Two peer sessions that have discovered each other over multicast
and then open and close repeatedly wedge forever inside session close:

    z_session_drop
      CloseBuilder::wait
        ZRuntime::block_in_place
          tokio block_on -> parked, never returns

with every zenoh runtime worker idle and nothing runnable. One process alone
never reproduces it; two do, usually within five cycles. The reproducer is a
20-line program that does nothing but open and close a default-config session in
a loop; it went upstream with the fix rather than living here.

WHERE IT ACTUALLY HANGS, found by instrumenting the close path rather than by
reading it: Runtime::close_inner, on its FIRST await.

    self.task_controller.terminate_all_async().await;   <-- never returns
    self.manager.close().await;                          <-- never reached

terminate_all_async() is

    self.tracker.close();
    self.token.cancel();
    self.tracker.wait().await

and has no timeout, so it waits for every tracked task to finish. The gossip
autoconnect handler spawns its peer-connect task with `spawn`, which is TRACKED
BUT NOT ABORTABLE -- `token.cancel()` does nothing to it. The task is sitting in
`connect_peer()` against a peer that is itself shutting down, so nothing ever
finishes it and close waits for ever.

`spawn_abortable` is the same facility one line up the file, and it exists for
exactly this: it wires the task to the controller's cancellation token so
terminate_all_async can actually terminate it. Aborting a peer-connect while the
runtime is closing is the correct outcome -- we are not going to use the
connection.

WHY THIS IS NOT eclipse-zenoh/zenoh#2637. That open PR fixes a real deadlock of
the same family -- `block_in_place` parking the routing `ctrl_lock` holder on a
tokio Mutex, under peer churn, which is our workload -- and it was the obvious
candidate. It was tried FIRST, in full, and measured: still hung 6 runs out of 8.
Its two hunks are in `terminate_peer_connector_zid`'s callers; ours is a
different task in the same file that never gets cancelled. Both are worth having
upstream, but only this one closes our hang. Carrying #2637 as well was measured
at 8/8 passing but slower to shut down (1-20 s against 1-4 s for this alone), so
it is not carried here.

MEASURED, two processes, 50 open/close cycles each:

    unpatched                        HUNG 3 of 3 runs, by cycle 3-12
    #2637 hunks only                 HUNG 6 of 8 runs, by cycle 3-9
    this patch only                  8 of 8 passed, 1-4 s
    this patch + #2637               8 of 8 passed, 1-20 s

APPLIED WITH PLAIN `git apply`, which fails loudly if the context has moved
rather than applying somewhere it should not, and third_party/zenoh-c.cmake
verifies afterwards that the change is actually present -- a patch step that
fails without stopping the configure would leave us silently building unpatched
zenoh, which is exactly the sort of thing this patch exists to prevent.

Upstream as eclipse-zenoh/zenoh#2733, still open. Drop this patch, and the block
in third_party/zenoh-c.cmake that applies it, once a zenoh-c release carries it.

